## Working with the code

- Namespace: `{{NAMESPACE}}`. Include project headers as `#include <{{INCLUDE_PREFIX}}/...>`.
- `version{{EXT.hxx}}` is generated by CMake from `version{{EXT.hxx}}.in` into the build tree. Edit the `.in` file, never the generated header. The version itself comes from `project(... VERSION ...)` in `CMakeLists.txt`.
- New source files go to the `SRCS` list and new test files to the `TEST_SRCS` list in the matching `CMakeLists.txt`.
{{#with.libs}}
- Put the logic into `libs/{{LIB_NAME}}`, where it can be tested{{#cmake.application}}; keep `libs/app` a thin entry point{{/cmake.application}}. A new library goes into its own `libs/<name>/` directory, added with `add_subdirectory()` in `libs/CMakeLists.txt`.
{{/with.libs}}
{{^with.conan}}
- Tests are built when `{{NAME_PREFIX}}_TESTING` is `ON` (off by default) and need GoogleTest to be findable with `find_package(GTest)`.
{{/with.conan}}
{{#with.conan}}
- New C++ dependencies go to `[requires]` in `conanfile.txt` and then `find_package()` + `target_link_libraries()` in CMake.
{{/with.conan}}
- Compiler flags for every target come from `{{NAME_PREFIX}}_ADDITIONAL_COMPILE_FLAGS` and `{{NAME_PREFIX}}_ADDITIONAL_LINK_FLAGS` set in `.flow/cmake/common.cmake`.
- Format changed C++ files with clang-format before committing.

{{/with.cmake}}
## Project Flow

- The proj-flow version is pinned in `PROJ_FLOW_VERSION` of `.flow/flow.py`. Additional Python packages needed by local extensions go to `REQUIREMENTS` in the same file.
- Steps come from the extensions listed in `extensions` of `.flow/config.yml`. A local extension under `.flow/extensions/` becomes active once its module is added there{{#with.cmake}} (for example `{{FLOW_PACKAGE}}.icons` for the Icons step, which needs ImageMagick){{/with.cmake}}.
- `./flow <command> --help` documents every command and its arguments.

{{#with.github.actions}}
## Continuous integration

`build.yml` asks `./flow github matrix` for the list of builds and runs `./flow run` on each of them. Keep the local matrix in `.flow/matrix.yml` and the CI matrix in sync by changing the matrix, not the workflow.
{{#with.github.auto-release}}

On each push to `main`, `./flow github release` reads the commits since the last tag, bumps the version, updates the changelog and drafts a GitHub release; the builds then attach their packages to it. Do not bump versions or edit the changelog by hand.
{{/with.github.auto-release}}
{{#with.github.no-auto-release}}

Releases are cut by hand: a maintainer runs `./flow github release`, which reads the commits since the last tag, bumps the version, updates the changelog and drafts a GitHub release. Pushing its `v*` tag starts `build.yml`, which attaches the packages and publishes the release. Do not bump versions or edit the changelog by hand.
{{/with.github.no-auto-release}}

{{/with.github.actions}}
## Commits

Commit nothing unless asked to.

{{#with.conventional-commits}}
Follow [COMMITS.md](COMMITS.md): commit messages use Conventional Commits, and the commit history is the source of version bumps and the changelog. Keep one logical change per commit.
{{/with.conventional-commits}}
{{^with.conventional-commits}}
Keep one logical change per commit, with a short subject line in the imperative mood.
{{#with.github.actions}}

`./flow github release` reads [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) types to decide on the version bump and the changelog: `feat:` bumps MINOR, `fix:` bumps PATCH, and `!` after the type (or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer) bumps MAJOR. Use these prefixes for changes that should be released.
{{/with.github.actions}}
{{/with.conventional-commits}}
